      "text_raw": "If no load capacitance is\npresent, but a large input\ncapacitance C , then the\nGS\nbandwidth is determined at\nthe input. This is also true\nfor many sensor and bio-\nmedical preamplifiers where\nthe source resistance can be\nquite high (>1 MV).\nIn this case, the BW is\nsimply given by the RC pro-\nduct at the input.\nThe GBW on the other\nhand, is not as simple as\nbefore. Many transistor\nparameters now play a role.\nSome of them can be bundled in the high-frequency parameter f .\nT\nFor high-frequency performance, it is not sufficient however to make f large. Rather the\nT\nproduct f r must be optimized, which is a technological challenge indeed!\nT DS\nThe channel length does not seem to play a role; both W and V −V must be made small!!!\nGS T\nAll surprising results indeed!",
      "text": "If no load capacitance is present, but a large input capacitance C , then the GS bandwidth is determined at the input. This is also true for many sensor and biomedical preamplifiers where the source resistance can be quite high (>1 MV). In this case, the BW is simply given by the RC product at the input. The GBW on the other hand, is not as simple as before. Many transistor parameters now play a role. Some of them can be bundled in the high-frequency parameter f . T For high-frequency performance, it is not sufficient however to make f large. Rather the T product f r must be optimized, which is a technological challenge indeed! T DS The channel length does not seem to play a role; both W and V −V must be made small!!! GS T All surprising results indeed!"
    }
  ],
